WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.500
GCP core services.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.743
Google Cloud maps neatly onto AWS, and a few pieces genuinely shine.

3
00:00:07.743 --> 00:00:08.833
Let's see them.

4
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:13.133
Start by mapping GCP from what you already know.

5
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:17.867
Compute Engine is the equivalent of EC2: plain virtual machines.

6
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:21.367
Cloud Run runs containers and scales them to zero.

7
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:27.800
GKE is widely seen as the strongest managed Kubernetes, because Google wrote Kubernetes.

8
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.678
And BigQuery is a serverless warehouse.

9
00:00:30.678 --> 00:00:31.933
No cluster to size.

10
00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:35.233
The data tooling is the calling card of GCP.

11
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:44.433
Many data-heavy teams pick GCP for BigQuery and its analytics stack, even when everything else lives on AWS.

12
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.633
Here is the whole pitch of Cloud Run.

13
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:51.467
With no traffic there are zero instances, and you pay nothing.

14
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:54.389
When traffic arrives, one instance starts.

15
00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:56.700
That first request pays a cold start.

16
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.300
Under a spike, it scales out by itself.

17
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.997
And afterwards it drops back to zero.

18
00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:03.033
Back to no cost.

19
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:05.700
Here it is in the terminal.

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.667
One command takes your source to a public HTTPS URL, with minimum instances set to zero.

21
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:14.469
Then we time a request.

22
00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:18.454
The first hit after idle takes about one point eight seconds.

23
00:01:18.454 --> 00:01:21.067
Warm hits take about forty milliseconds.

24
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:24.400
And BigQuery runs a query with no cluster at all.

25
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.567
You pay per amount of data scanned.

26
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.000
Here's a real use.

27
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.447
Your public exoplanet demo runs on Cloud Run.

28
00:01:32.447 --> 00:01:35.733
Weeks of almost no traffic cost almost nothing.

29
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:39.500
Then a Hacker News spike arrives, and the service scales itself.

30
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.920
Meanwhile you load the full archive into BigQuery and run analytics with no warehouse to look after.

31
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.367
And measure the cold start yourself, so you can say two seconds, then forty milliseconds.

32
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:53.767
Let's recap.

33
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:56.567
Map GCP by analogy from AWS.

34
00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:00.200
Cloud Run scales to zero, and costs nothing while idle.

35
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.267
GKE is top-tier managed Kubernetes.

36
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:06.849
And BigQuery is serverless, paying per scan.

37
00:02:06.849 --> 00:02:08.267
Next up: Vertex AI.
